1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,890
Two averages, two standard deviations, and a correlation of minus point oh six.

2
00:00:06,390 --> 00:00:10,390
What shape is the cloud, and which way does its best-fit line tilt?

3
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:16,683
You probably pictured a shapeless oval, with a nearly flat line.

4
00:00:17,183 --> 00:00:18,663
Here are the real points.

5
00:00:19,763 --> 00:00:24,383
A dinosaur, drawn by Alberto Cairo, with these same five numbers.

6
00:00:25,033 --> 00:00:31,383
In a review of seven hundred three physiology papers, eighty-six percent used bar graphs:

7
00:00:31,783 --> 00:00:33,683
an average and an error bar.

8
00:00:34,183 --> 00:00:36,443
Only thirteen percent showed the points.

9
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:39,013
How much can five numbers hide?

10
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:45,243
Let's hide a star in them, and demand that all five match the dinosaur's to two decimals.

11
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:50,170
The first version slides every point onto its own spot on a star.

12
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:53,763
We got the star, and lost all five numbers.

13
00:00:54,663 --> 00:00:59,973
The center moved, the x spread grew, and the y spread shrank by almost a quarter.

14
00:01:00,766 --> 00:01:03,456
So what exactly do five numbers pin down?

15
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:08,320
The two averages mark a single point: the cloud's balance point.

16
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:13,503
The two standard deviations reach out on each side, and draw a box.

17
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:16,456
Many ellipses touch all four walls.

18
00:01:16,656 --> 00:01:18,876
The correlation, r, picks one.

19
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:21,793
Say r is point five.

20
00:01:22,293 --> 00:01:24,603
Go one standard deviation right.

21
00:01:25,003 --> 00:01:28,593
On average, the cloud rises r times ess why.

22
00:01:29,293 --> 00:01:33,933
That's the best-fit line, through the middle of every slice of the ellipse.

23
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:37,763
At the wall, the slices shrink to the touch point.

24
00:01:38,163 --> 00:01:40,993
For the dinosaur, it's just below the middle.

25
00:01:41,666 --> 00:01:44,546
That ellipse is everything the five numbers know.

26
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,260
Now replay the first version, and watch the ellipse.

27
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,030
How would you fix it?

28
00:01:52,966 --> 00:01:54,926
Slide it until the centers meet.

29
00:01:55,226 --> 00:01:57,346
Stretch it until the boxes match.

30
00:01:57,846 --> 00:01:59,786
Then tip it, keeping the box,

31
00:01:59,986 --> 00:02:02,446
until it touches the walls at the same height.

32
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:06,210
All five match now, to every decimal place.

33
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,150
But add a sixth number: the median of y.

34
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:12,280
Our star misses it by six.

35
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:17,846
In 2017, Justin Matejka and George Fitzmaurice built a search.

36
00:02:18,246 --> 00:02:22,546
It keeps these five numbers fixed while it nudges points toward a target.

37
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:26,580
From the dinosaur they grew twelve shapes: this circle,

38
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:28,240
this star,

39
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:30,100
this bullseye,

40
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:31,770
this X,

41
00:02:32,570 --> 00:02:33,780
and these stripes.

42
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:37,310
Each one has this same ellipse, and a different median.

43
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:43,150
Francis Anscombe made the same point in 1973, with eleven points.

44
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,100
Same rule: ess ex across,

45
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:48,970
r times ess why up.

46
00:02:49,933 --> 00:02:51,563
So the slope is the rise

47
00:02:51,863 --> 00:02:52,863
over the run.

48
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:57,570
All four of his datasets share these five numbers.

49
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:02,170
A trend, a curve, an outlier, and one point doing all the work.

50
00:03:04,133 --> 00:03:09,243
Your line was right: five numbers fix it exactly, slope minus point one.

51
00:03:10,043 --> 00:03:15,003
Your cloud was wrong: a blur around this ellipse, all that five numbers can draw.

52
00:03:15,603 --> 00:03:19,543
Most of those papers used a bar graph, which draws only two.

53
00:03:20,143 --> 00:03:22,843
The dinosaur lived in everything they leave out.

